However, task-specific adapters must be selected at deployment, and the oracle task ID is typically unavailable. Instead, the robot receives the current observation and language instruction, from which the appropriate task-specific adapter must be identified. Recent methods [12], [13] dynamically select task-specific adapters from the current input during execution. By design, the adapters and selection modules therefore remain active in the control loop, increasing GPU memory and per-step computation as tasks accumulate. As illustrated in Fig. 1, this accumulation appears along two axes. During training, task-specific adapters, selection modules, and associated state accumulate on disk as new tasks are learned. At deployment, the adapters and selection modules must remain available on the GPU and are used throughout the control loop. Both axes therefore grow with the task stream, conflicting with the fixed resource budgets of onboard robot hardware.Can a VLA policy learn new tasks continually while keeping its deployment cost independent of the task stream and limiting task-dependent storage growth? To address this, we propose SARAM, a lifelong robot learning method that addresses the two axes at their respective stages: storage as tasks accumulate, and deployment cost at execution. During learning, each new task first reuses a linear combination of frozen LoRA adapters from previous tasks, then adds new adapters only at layers selected under a sparsity penalty. A duplication penalty discourages the new adapters from reproducing what the reused library already
