expresses. The newly allocated adapters and combination coefficients are frozen after training, so later tasks cannot overwrite previously learned parameters. Because every pertask update stays linear in the weights, it can later be folded into the backbone rather than evaluated as a separate module. SARAM then removes task selection from the control loop. After each task is learned, the demonstrations are passed through the adapter-free backbone and its layerwise activation statistics are summarized into a compact task signature. These frozen-backbone statistics provide taskdependent signatures without requiring a learned selection module. At deployment, the initial observation and instruction are summarized in the same way, matched against the stored signatures, and the selected update is merged into the backbone before execution. The episode therefore runs on a single backbone, with no task-specific adapter or selection module inside the control loop, keeping GPU memory and per-step inference cost at the backbone’s level regardless of the number of learned tasks. We evaluate SARAM on four LIBERO suites against adapter-based methods [12], [13] and standard continual learning baselines. SARAM attains the highest final success rate and AUC on all four suites while requiring up to 4.5× less on-disk state per task than adapter-based baselines. At deployment, SARAM remains at the backbone’s level in both GPU memory and control frequency: after ten tasks it runs at 15.2 Hz with 748 MB of GPU memory, identical to the backbone alone. By contrast, CLARE and DMPEL grow to 922 MB and 829 MB of GPU memory and slow to 10.1 Hz and 5.0 Hz after ten tasks, respectively. On a seven-task realrobot stream with a different VLA backbone, SARAM ends the stream at the same success rate it reached when each task was learned, showing that the method transfers to a physical robot and a different backbone architecture. We summarize our contributions as follows:
•We propose SARAM, an adapter-based lifelong robot learning method that limits per-task storage through adapter reuse, sparse allocation, and compact task signatures in place of learned selection modules. •At deployment, task signatures move selection outside the control loop, and the corresponding task update is merged into the backbone to keep GPU memory and per-step inference cost independent of the number of learned tasks. •On four LIBERO suites, SARAM attains the highest final success rate and AUC while requiring up to 4.5× less on-disk state per task, and real-robot experiments with a different VLA backbone demonstrate applicability beyond simulation.
II. RELATED WORK
Lifelong Robot Learning.Lifelong robot learning [1], [15] studies how robots can learn new tasks sequentially while retaining those learned before. A central challenge, shared with continual learning in general [3], is catastrophic forgetting [6], where learning new tasks degrades performance
on earlier ones. Existing approaches address this problem through replay, regularization, or architectural isolation. Replay methods retain past demonstrations for later training [4], which incurs storage overhead and can be undesirable when past demonstrations cannot be retained. Regularization methods such as EWC [6] avoid storing past data but often provide weaker protection against forgetting. Architectural-isolation methods such as PackNet [7] protect previous tasks by reserving separate capacity, but the available capacity becomes increasingly constrained as more tasks are learned. In robot manipulation, LIBERO [1] provides a standard benchmark for evaluating sequential task learning and retention.
Adapter-Based Lifelong Robot Learning.With the emergence of large pretrained VLA policies [8]–[10], [16], [17], recent work has increasingly adopted lightweight adapters for task-specific adaptation while keeping the backbone frozen [11]–[13]. After each task is learned, its adapter is frozen, preventing later training from overwriting previously learned task parameters. This provides strong retention, but requires maintaining additional task-specific parameters as the task stream grows. When the oracle task ID is unavailable, CLARE [12] and DMPEL [13] dynamically select or combine task-specific modules from the current input using learned discriminators and a router, respectively. These inputdependent mechanisms remain active during control, causing task-specific storage and deployment cost to grow with the task stream.
Resource-Efficient Continual Learning.Recent work beyond robotics has explored reducing the parameter growth of adapter-based continual learning. Some approaches compress task-specific adapters into a shared low-rank representation or merge multiple task updates into a shared model [18]– [20]. These methods reduce parameter growth, but typically update shared parameters that also represent previously learned tasks. Their evaluation settings typically do not consider task selection from observations and instructions at deployment. In contrast, SARAM keeps previously learned task parameters frozen, reuses past adapters through combination coefficients, and adds new adapters only at layers retained by sparse allocation. It further replaces learned selection modules with task signatures and merges the corresponding task update into the VLA backbone before execution, so only the backbone runs during control.
