B. Overview
SARAM addresses the two growth axes at their respective stages: disk storage during learning, and GPU cost at deployment. Each new task first reuses the frozen adapters of past tasks (Sec. III-D). A sparse adapter allocation then adds new capacity only at layers selected under a sparsity penalty (Sec. III-E), and a duplication penalty keeps that capacity from re-expressing what the reused adapters already provide (Sec. III-F). At deployment, every per-task update is linear in the weights, and the task is selected once per episode from frozen-backbone activation statistics (Sec. IIIG). The selected update can therefore be added directly to the backbone weights, and the episode runs on a single backbone (Sec. III-H), holding GPU memory and per-step inference cost at the backbone’s own level.
