IV. EXPERIMENTS
Our experiments address three questions. (i) Does SARAM retain previously learned tasks while acquiring new ones without the oracle task ID (Sec. IV-B, IV-E)? (ii) Does it limit on-disk growth and keep GPU memory and control frequency at the backbone’s level as tasks accumulate (Sec. IV-C)? (iii) Which components of SARAM account for its performance (Sec. IV-D)?
A. Simulation Experimental Setup
Benchmark and protocol.We evaluate on the four LIBERO suites [1]: Long, Goal, Spatial, and Object, each a stream ofT=10 tasks learned sequentially. Following the LIBERO protocol, each task is probed with rollout episodes at regular intervals during training, and the best checkpoint is deployed as the task’s final policy. Every method runs three seeds under a shared schedule: 10k training steps per task, 20-episode probes every 800 steps, and 40 episodes for each retention evaluation. For SARAM, the 10k-step budget includes all three phases. Retention evaluations use each method’s own task selection without the oracle task ID. Metrics.Lets j,t be the success rate of taskτ t evaluated after the stream has learnedτ 1 throughτ j. Let  ̄ts and ˆts be the mean and best success rate of the rollout probes recorded whileτ t itself trains with known task identity; the best checkpoint is the one deployed. We report the LIBERO metrics [1]: FWT= 1
