D. Ablations and Analysis
Component ablations.Table II examines each component of SARAM on LIBERO-Long. Without coefficient pre-fit, we skip Phase 1 and train the combination coefficients only with the new adapters in Phase 3. This lowers FWT and AUC, suggesting that fitting reuse first helps isolate the residual for new adapters. Inrandom allocation per task, we keep the same number of adapters as SARAM but randomly allocate their locations for each task. This causes the largest AUC drop and substantially higher variation across seeds. Infixed random allocation, one randomly sampled layer set is shared by all tasks, recovering much of this loss and suggesting that shared adapter locations facilitate library reuse. Infixed first-task allocation, the gate-selected layers of the first task are reused throughout the stream. Its lower AUC and higher variation than the full method indicate that task-specific gate fitting provides additional gains. Without library reuse, we setc (t)=0, preventing new tasks from using adapters learned for previous tasks. The resulting drop in FWT and AUC shows that the frozen library provides useful capacity under the same sparse allocation budget. Finally, without the duplication penalty, we setλ=0 during Phase 3. This lowers mean FWT and AUC and substantially increases variation across seeds, indicating that the penalty improves both performance and stability.
Where adapters are allocated.Figure 4 shows the layers that survive gate-based allocation on LIBERO-Long for each task and seed. Within each seed, tasks share many allocated layers under the common gate initialization, while taskspecific gradients lead to differences at a few locations, and no two tasks end with an identical layer set. Across seeds, the selected layers overlap little despite similar performance, suggesting that multiple sparse placements can be effective. Together with Table II, these results show that shared adapter locations facilitate library reuse, while fitting the gates for each task provides additional gains and stability. Effect ofξandλ.Table III examines how the sparsitypenalty weightξand duplication-penalty weightλaffect performance and storage. Increasingξreduces the number of stored parameters at the cost of continual-learning performance, showing that it controls the trade-offbetween
