E. Real-Robot Experiments
Real-robot setup.We evaluate on a UR5 robot with a Robotiq 2F-85 gripper, an external camera, and a wrist camera. The robot learns the seven tasks in Fig. 5 sequentially. Each task has about 50 teleoperated demonstrations and is evaluated with 10 rollouts. We useπ 0 [9] as the backbone with rank-8 LoRA candidates on its linear layers andξ=40, which keeps a similar fraction of layers per task as on LIBERO; other settings follow the simulation setup. We compare against SeqLoRA, ER, and per-task LoRA policies selected with the oracle task ID as a reference. Real-robot results.Table IV and Fig. 6 show the results over the seven-task stream. SeqLoRA retains only the last task, and ER, despite replaying past demonstrations, loses most earlier tasks as the stream grows. SARAM learns tasks close to the per-task reference and keeps them: its final success equals its success at learning time, with per-task differences within one episode. Task selection is correct on every heldout episode, including the same-scene die and cup tasks (Table V). These results match the LIBERO experiments and show that the framework transfers to a physical robot with a different VLA backbone.


