V. CONCLUSION
We presented SARAM, a lifelong robot learning method for VLA policies that limits storage growth during learning and removes task-specific computation from the control loop. Each new task reuses the frozen adapters of past tasks, adds new adapters only at layers selected by a sparsity penalty, and trains them under a duplication penalty. At deployment, a task signature built from frozen-backbone activations selects the task once per episode without the oracle task ID or a learned selection module, and the selected update is merged into the backbone so that only the backbone runs in the control loop. On four LIBERO suites, SARAM attains the highest SRfinal and AUC of all compared methods while adding up to 4.5×less on-disk state per task than adapterbased baselines, and its GPU memory and control frequency stay at the backbone’s level while the adapter-based baselines slow down by up to 3×. On a seven-task real-robot stream with a different backbone, it learns and retains all seven tasks throughout the stream.
